unn–Minkowski inequality for every $0\\lt p\\lt 1$. Combined with Saroglou's transfer theorem and a support-subspace reduction, it yields the logarithmic inequality for every even log-concave Radon measure and the scalar-dilation $(B)$-conjecture.\n\nResult family 091: Logarithmic and L_{p} Brunn–Minkowski inequalities and the B-conjecture\n\nThe main result is listed as formalized in Lean.\n\nThis manuscript appears in the repository’s main-result formalization catalogue. The scope below identifies the covered statements and any limitations.\n\nThe logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski conjecture asserts that logarithmic interpolation of origin-symmetric convex bodies preserves the geometric-mean lower bound for volume. The formalized result proves, for every dimension $n\\ge1$, such bodies $K,L\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, and $0\\le t\\le1$, that their logarithmic Wulff combination has volume at least $|K|^{1-t}|L|^t$. It assumes neither boundary smoothness nor coordinatewise unconditionality.\n\nRead this scope note on GitHub ↗\n\nLogBrunnMinkowski.lean\n\nFamily 092·23 pages\n\nLean: main result formalized ↗\n\n### A single-lattice covering bound of order n log n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeficit functions and the log Sobolev inequality - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022123626002922)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ## Journal of Functional Analysis ... Research article ... There is a rich theory of entropy monotonicity for the heat equation that links PDE, probability, statistical mechanics, information theory, and a number of sharp functional inequalities, see, e.g., [4], [16], [27]. ... Theorem 0.10 will show that the high-dimensional limit of the elliptic deficit {d}_{0} gives the parabolic deficit {D}_{0} and the elliptic equation for {d}_{0} converges to the parabolic equation for {D}_{0}.\nWe introduce two deficit functions, one elliptic and one parabolic, then show that the parabolic deficit is pointwise the limit of the elliptic and, that the elliptic satisfies an equation that converges to the equation for the parabolic. These pointwise quantities and their equations recover the monotonicities and lead to an elliptic proof of the log Sobolev inequality as well as new concentration of measure phenomena.\n\n## Keywords\n\nLog Sobolev inequality\n\n;\n\nGradient estimate\n\n;\n\nHarmonic function\n\n;\n\nHeat equation\n\n  * Previous article in this issue\n  * Next article in this issue\n\n## 0. Introduction\n\nThere is a rich theory of entropy monotonicity for the heat equation that links PDE, probability, statistical mechanics, information theory, and a number of sharp functional inequalities, see, e.g., [4], [16], [27]. The link comes from regarding a positive solution u of the heat equation as a probability measure and studying its evolution over time. There are a number of monotonicity formulas, starting with the Shannon-Boltzmann entropy and its derivative, the Fisher information. \nMonotonicity of \\mathcal{W} for the heat equation can be deduced from (0.4) together with (0.5), but can also be seen from (0.11), (0.12) by taking the limit as the dimension . The logarithmic Sobolev inequality can be deduced from (0.4), (0.5) and the central limit theorem, but it can also be seen from (0.11), (0.12) together with concentration of measures in high dimensions. Equation (0.13) links asymptotically the infinitesimal versions of the two monotonicities.\n\nBustamante and Reiris, [6], showed that the parabolic monotonicity followed from the elliptic. Their result was, in a sense, an integrated version of what we show here. In their argument, the manifolds need to be closed. In contrast, our arguments are local. We show that the quantities here satisfy differential equations and we can therefore allow non-compact manifolds like Euclidean space. In fact, the formulas we show here reveal a relationship between the tw